<article class="env-remark" id="02BR">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02BR">Remark <span data-tag="02BR">3.1.6.10</span>.</a> We can describe the strict bicategory $\operatorname {h}_{2} \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$ more informally as follows: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The objects of $\operatorname {h}_{2} \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$ are Kan complexes. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The morphisms of $\operatorname {h}_{2} \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$ are morphisms of Kan complexes $f: X \rightarrow Y$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $f_0, f_1: X \rightarrow Y$ are morphisms of Kan complexes, then a $2$-morphism $f_0 \Rightarrow f_1$ in $\operatorname {h}_{2} \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$ is an equivalence class of homotopies $h: \Delta ^1 \times X \rightarrow Y$ from $f_0 = h|_{\{ 0\}  \times X}$ to $f_1 = h|_{\{ 1\}  \times X}$, where we regard $h$ and $h'$ as equivalent if they are homotopic relative to $\operatorname{\partial \Delta }^1 \times X$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
